#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063964 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "4f78dc34-7cc7-5024-9641-b4ea74e4d155" :question/math-academy-id "e-3743" :question/problem "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "088bc8a4-e82a-586b-89b5-49482f579c4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3744" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efa54de-aaac-5dff-9339-d60a5ec4bbf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293829} {:answer/id #uuid "976d869b-eb2d-50d8-a0c1-d9a57a688a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293830} {:answer/id #uuid "2de86e8b-0bf7-52d2-b487-a882601ff09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293831} {:answer/id #uuid "79d6c248-2d4a-59f5-a62c-a8828d4548b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293832} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0e7ba3-d268-5081-84a2-16d15be885eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79d6c248-2d4a-59f5-a62c-a8828d4548b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293832} :answer-field/id #uuid "495bd8d2-b59c-5fff-81a2-2e8195513d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c703bb5-c256-594b-9d43-e00104658951" :question/math-academy-id "q-114414" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=-1<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(0)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293836} {:answer/id #uuid "be64c842-1c2a-5e23-a999-aa07cfad96f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293837} {:answer/id #uuid "5c808dd9-e862-5d77-b630-69b56e7db2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293838} {:answer/id #uuid "381f926c-73d2-55a8-b374-e171f87db6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293839} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf16a51-e154-5a7d-95d8-b1d2a62fdb49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293836} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29de4f2-d3d7-5c89-9a1e-6f0bd70b60fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "625bbe54-0b3f-5afb-b15e-eb3a5b309cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-114415" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7799333c-5cab-556b-8064-b1a628ef6985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293843} {:answer/id #uuid "ae68b24d-8c35-5402-a0ac-ff5ccea754bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293844} {:answer/id #uuid "67e6f828-2dd2-5df9-9b1e-e3a4747b4e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293845} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c8ed34-1777-5cdc-9de1-9d7ec5b6e7fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293846} {:answer/id #uuid "a4235445-4dfd-5cde-b68b-d83e48b0ce6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4235445-4dfd-5cde-b68b-d83e48b0ce6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293847} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b6f0d46-1560-5013-8ce9-928e6a66d630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "294cdcbe-5050-50a2-a47c-f7aeafc041f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-21536" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-12(1)+2 \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ded46d81-de7b-5a0f-a1a8-e56a8aed4c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293850} {:answer/id #uuid "689fc39e-7cfa-5db6-ae56-442342da7e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293851} {:answer/id #uuid "94abf5d7-a5f3-584b-b859-8ef5a165ef5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293852} {:answer/id #uuid "81347035-635c-52bb-b6ac-c0f221947a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293853} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba94071-22dc-51ae-b4e8-2cdf75d755ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "689fc39e-7cfa-5db6-ae56-442342da7e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293851} :answer-field/id #uuid "edaa049e-80e5-58e9-a5c1-d731e198929a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "968678fd-66ab-55fd-a098-de84b2a8bf2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-22007" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x+3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6x-12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =6\\cdot 2-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}-12(1)+12=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=2$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e673bbe-fcba-589a-b2e1-30a4b18b5db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293857} {:answer/id #uuid "3103819b-1728-56ee-8ac5-1f6e1198e365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293858} {:answer/id #uuid "6332005e-56e9-5a5a-959c-5bff67214268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293859} {:answer/id #uuid "4e227169-b9e0-5d16-b286-ac8d9ddbf39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293860} {:answer/id #uuid "06a55726-014d-5bc7-972e-837ac72e3e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3103819b-1728-56ee-8ac5-1f6e1198e365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293858} :answer-field/id #uuid "2521b54c-4fcf-5186-956d-ebac9b150499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c59ed93-a9e6-53ac-bc6c-4a594d69961a" :question/math-academy-id "q-22040" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3/2}-12{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}$ defined on $(0,\\infty ).$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3/2}-12{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2}-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{-1/2}+3\\cdot {1}^{-3/2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {1}^{-5/2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{-3/2}(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point on $(0,\\infty )$ is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3/2}{((\\frac{1}{2})-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3/2}{((-\\frac{1}{2}))}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{{2}^{-3/2}})(\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\cdot {2}^{-2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3\\cdot {2}^{-3/2}(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{-3/2}>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=1$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab149a10-203f-5fe4-b068-d03658862a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293864} {:answer/id #uuid "c86cdcb4-2b42-5100-af95-d4f2aebe7b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293865} {:answer/id #uuid "b874912d-6bf3-580f-8aba-051088a7954b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293866} {:answer/id #uuid "227c0b5b-7b59-5a02-bb53-72c0dee4bb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293867} {:answer/id #uuid "92fc53c7-6663-5a16-950c-e9f8156bb887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "227c0b5b-7b59-5a02-bb53-72c0dee4bb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293867} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9209931-a51d-54ed-9582-c8964f74802c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ca1bbcd-ffa9-5f94-8065-66d16ed26b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-22045" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-4x+10.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-4x+10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-4=4(x-1)^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$   we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1-1)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =4(0-1)^{3}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4(2-1)^{3}=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative changes the sign at  $x=1$ from minus to plus, we conclude that $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df5ad58-4f62-57a6-8461-6d77ae1ab275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293871} {:answer/id #uuid "aa41b0cf-7574-5c12-951a-a9bb7e07690d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293872} {:answer/id #uuid "b4099d23-a41b-5b41-a92d-304aacb8c55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293873} {:answer/id #uuid "96082e02-a73a-5241-9a3f-1490cbff74d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293874} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d8cab0-e906-545f-9478-c522a9586b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4099d23-a41b-5b41-a92d-304aacb8c55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5100d93-dced-5733-8361-e7433b86c347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4be2768-0797-558d-b2df-d9686629ebc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-22079" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is zero, so we should apply the first derivative test.\n\nLet's evaluate the first derivative at auxiliary points $\\pm 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=0,$ $f(x)$ has no relative extrema.\n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)=0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc367a49-bbe1-53b2-b92f-9665a6e0a876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293878} {:answer/id #uuid "de10b173-78aa-5bb6-8877-3b9e366c4ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293879} {:answer/id #uuid "3df6c5b8-141e-5342-b3d1-b04e81ab9f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293880} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c6e5f7-7b25-5d62-9884-0e6a1ae25cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293881} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4bbc1e-7ae2-52f0-855b-87476628a903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df6c5b8-141e-5342-b3d1-b04e81ab9f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293880} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d34f65d-1c29-54b6-987f-68e2c7328b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56b8e08e-9c93-5ed9-8c5c-762aa5f29750" :question/math-academy-id "q-22165" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =4(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1)^{3}(-1-2)=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1)^{3}(1-2)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =(3)^{3}(3-2)=27>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e099d05-93f3-54cd-b4d7-4798641df132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061842} {:answer/id #uuid "dacbda70-1377-52f6-a5e7-055341259beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061843} {:answer/id #uuid "e23211cb-1730-56f0-803d-e4c1bcebc97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061844} {:answer/id #uuid "356ef0f6-7046-542b-bad0-849d97dc9474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061845} {:answer/id #uuid "41ec81cf-9788-59de-a704-623a4cc3969c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e099d05-93f3-54cd-b4d7-4798641df132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061842} :answer-field/id #uuid "6153a82b-a3cf-5d0b-abfd-d8c3da44ff45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4e43127-8c70-53b8-9c7f-f4ea673dfe39" :question/math-academy-id "q-2459" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III and VI are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf3e699-bc6b-5488-9a35-7f0dad173e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293885} {:answer/id #uuid "9279ea72-feed-5421-993c-a268dffbef6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293886} {:answer/id #uuid "8568e6fa-b37b-56c3-9072-5b4026280932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293887} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf42a90-a6b5-5cc5-8579-62ac39894a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293888} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddc6cc5-7a41-5fb6-8645-984c16b29111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9279ea72-feed-5421-993c-a268dffbef6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293886} :answer-field/id #uuid "568c55d3-5794-53fc-a578-82d8ab32d9fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d62b37ed-618d-5468-9b93-2debfd8c89e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2469" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}]}
